Alembic migrations for ReckonAI. Single database, one linear history.

Two things differ from a stock Alembic setup, both deliberate:

  - The database URL is read from reckonai.config.Settings, not from
    alembic.ini, whose sqlalchemy.url is left empty. See env.py.
  - Migrations run on Render through the web service's preDeployCommand, never
    from a start command. See specs/2026-11-07-repo-deployment-skeleton
    requirements.md D7.

Because pre-deploy runs while the previous version is still serving traffic, a
migration must be backward compatible with the code it is replacing for the
length of the overlap window. Expand, then contract: add a column, deploy the
code that writes it, drop the old one in a later revision. Dropping or renaming
a column the running instance still selects will break it.

`alembic check` is part of the merge gate (Phase 2 onward). It fails when a
model has changed without a matching revision, which is otherwise invisible
until the next deploy applies a revision that no longer matches the code being
deployed. tests/test_migrations.py runs it as a test too.

Autogenerate is a starting point, not an answer. Read a generated revision
before trusting it, and check in particular that every CHECK constraint is
present and named, that a composite foreign key carries both of its columns,
that NUMERIC kept its precision and scale rather than becoming bare, and that
downgrade() drops in dependency order. A lost CHECK leaves every ORM-level test
passing while the database accepts the row it was meant to refuse.

Do not introduce a native Postgres enum type without handling its lifecycle by
hand. Autogenerate does not reliably emit the type's CREATE/DROP alongside the
table's, so a downgrade leaves an orphaned type and the next upgrade fails on a
duplicate. Phase 2 used a VARCHAR plus a CHECK constraint for
order.disbursement_mode for this reason.

A model module that is not imported by reckonai/models/__init__.py is absent
from Base.metadata, and autogenerate reads an absent table as a table to DROP.
Add new model modules there.

Usage:

  uv run alembic upgrade head
  uv run alembic downgrade base
  uv run alembic check                   # models and migrations agree
  uv run alembic revision --autogenerate -m "<message>"
  uv run alembic upgrade head --sql      # review the SQL without connecting
